    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The human immune system utilizes T lymphocytes to distinguish between self and non-self to protect the host against pathogens. This same process can lead to recognition of transplanted organs as foreign, and initiates an immune response against the graft. Thus, immunosuppression, which primarily targets cell-mediated immunity by T cells, becomes crucial for graft survival. Optimal T cell activation requires signal 1 that results from the ligation of antigen-specific T cell receptor (TCR) and cognate peptide bound to major histocompatibility complex (MHC) proteins on antigen presenting cells (APC), as well as signal 2, that can be either a negative or positive co-stimulatory signal from APC. Initiating positive co-stimulatory signals when bound by CD28 on naïve T cells, B7-1 (CD80) and B7-2 (CD86) on AOC are rapidly upregulated upon APC activation, even though their up-regulation timing and signaling mechanisms may differ. CTLA-4 is a B7 family member that competitively inhibits CD28 by binding to B7 with greater affinity, thus producing negative co-stimulatory signals and reducing the immune response.1,2 Abatacept is a is a CTLA4-Ig fusion protein that has been used to prolong graft survival by interfering with signal 2 delivered by the B7/CD28 interaction. However, previous work may have oversimplified the outcome of the CD28:B7 co-stimulatory pathway as binary (activate vs. inactivate) instead of combinatorial. In this study we address the quantitative and qualitative effects of the B7/CD28 pathway on regulatory T cells (Treg) development and maintenance by selective blockade of CD80 and CD86.  We hypothesized that selective blockade of either CD80 or CD86 molecules can result in different degrees of immune regulation and Treg cells. Using a mouse heterotopic heart model of Treg-dependent long-term graft survival, we take immunomodulatory approaches that spare CD80 signaling to promote Treg development. After differentially treating mice with anti-CD80 and anti-CD86 blocking antibodies and CTLA4-Ig, we confirmed that CTLA4-Ig treatment results in accelerated graft rejection and a reduction in Helios+ Treg, a putative marker of natural Treg.  We further show that combinatorial (anti-CD80+anti-CD86) approaches suppress both effector memory T cells and Tregs, while single monoclonal antibody (mAb) treatment of anti-CD86 mAb alone or anti-CD80 mAb alone results in a variable population of Treg and T effector cells, depending on the timing and the degree of surface molecule expression. Therefore, selective blockade of B7 molecules may be useful for promoting expansion of Treg cells in transplantation, although their optimal induction time and magnitude still needs further research.",
